WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:03.360
When you hear the word “bond”, what comes
to mind?

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:06.360
Sean Connery, the best Bond, duh.

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:09.090
Although Pierce Brosnan was pretty good too...

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:14.259
What about the $20 savings bonds your grandmother
always gave you on your birthdays?

00:00:14.259 --> 00:00:15.259
Oh yeah!

00:00:15.259 --> 00:00:17.390
They’re probably gathering dust in our closet.

00:00:17.390 --> 00:00:19.609
I guess I never cashed those in!

00:00:19.609 --> 00:00:21.369
Well Never Say Never Again!

00:00:21.369 --> 00:00:25.449
To be honest, I didn’t really understand
what bonds were or how they worked until I

00:00:25.449 --> 00:00:27.419
became a financial professional.

00:00:27.419 --> 00:00:31.819
When used correctly, bonds can actually function
as a bit of a middle ground between the safety

00:00:31.819 --> 00:00:34.430
of cash and the wild swings of stocks.

00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:37.600
Oh I like it...kind of like a Daniel Craig.

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:39.960
Less misogyny, but still got that swagger.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:41.609
I’d like to learn more.

00:00:41.609 --> 00:00:45.440
For Your Eyes Only, here’s the Two Cents
guide to Bonds…

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:46.820
Savings Bonds.

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:04.960
Let’s start with the basics.

00:01:04.970 --> 00:01:08.790
You’ve probably borrowed money a time or
two in your life, whether it’s bumming ten

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:11.420
bucks from a friend or taking out a mortgage
on a house.

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:16.950
Most of the time we find ourselves in the
lendEE position, aka, the ones needing to

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:17.950
borrow money.

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:22.320
But how would you like to be the LendER,
charging interest and receiving payments?

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:24.990
That’s essentially what being a bond investor
is.

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:30.049
When a government or company needs to borrow
money, it does so by issuing bonds.

00:01:30.049 --> 00:01:33.920
Cities, states and counties issue bonds to
build roads and schools.

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:37.210
Corporations issue bonds to expand or get
through lean times.

00:01:37.210 --> 00:01:40.200
Let’s say you’ve got $10,000 you want
to invest.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:45.299
You decide to buy a $10,000, 10 year bond
at 4% interest.

00:01:45.299 --> 00:01:50.560
So you hand over your money, and the issuer
(either a Municipality, or Company) will pay

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:56.760
you a “coupon” of $200 every six months
coming to a grand total of $400/yr.

00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:03.620
And as long as Tomorrow Never Dies, after
10 years, you also get your original $10,000

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:04.659
back!

00:02:04.659 --> 00:02:07.439
There is another bond type that works a little
differently.

00:02:07.439 --> 00:02:11.780
This is the “zero-coupon bond”, like Series EE Savings Bonds.

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:15.520
These are initially sold to an investor below
their face value.

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:18.640
Those are the ones that gathered dust in our
closets for so long.

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:23.230
Our grandmas probably paid $10 for a bond
valued at $20.

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:28.000
The bond is guaranteed to be redeemable at
that final value after a certain number of

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:29.000
years.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:36.100
And that’s the golden word: “Guaranteed”:
By default, bonds are guaranteed by the issuer

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:38.970
to repay both principal AND interest.

00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:44.510
Unlike the Casino Royale that is the stock
market, bonds offer a Quantum of Solace to

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:47.240
investors seeking predictable returns.

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:49.280
Less “shaken”, more “stirred.”

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:54.300
However, while they are less risky than stocks,
you can still lose money in two different

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:58.930
ways; either through “issuer default”
or “market changes.”

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:04.170
Issuer Default occurs if the company or government
is unable to pay back the interest or principal.

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:10.300
Like when Puerto Rico defaulted on the $800
million owed to bond holders back in 2016.

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:15.800
The risk of this happening depends on the
issuer’s “credit rating”; from AAA on

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:20.940
the very low risk end to sub-prime, or junk
bonds, on the riskier side.

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:24.790
Basically, the better the score, the safer
your moneypenny.

00:03:24.790 --> 00:03:28.930
But some investors actually like companies
with lower ratings.

00:03:28.930 --> 00:03:29.930
Why?

00:03:29.930 --> 00:03:30.960
Higher interest!

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:31.970
YOLT!

00:03:31.970 --> 00:03:38.700
On average, sub-prime or “high yield”
bonds with lower credit ratings pay 6-8% more

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:41.470
interest than investment-grade bonds.

00:03:41.470 --> 00:03:49.000
If The World Is Not Enough for you, some sub-prime
bonds can even pay interest above 100% per year.

00:03:49.460 --> 00:03:53.830
Keep in mind that these are going to be offered
by companies and institutions that are probably

00:03:53.830 --> 00:03:56.180
on the edge of solvency.

00:03:56.180 --> 00:04:01.400
Unless you have a Live and Let Die attitude,
don’t gamble too much on this spin of the wheel.

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:07.480
The second way your bond can lose value is
due to changes within the bond market itself.

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:12.280
Some bonds will be bought and sold by multiple
buyers throughout their life, and what people

00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:17.479
are willing to pay for them depends on where
interest rates are, and how close to they

00:04:17.479 --> 00:04:18.949
are to maturity.

00:04:18.949 --> 00:04:24.939
So when the bond market is up, you might spend
a $1,200 for a bond that you’d later only

00:04:24.939 --> 00:04:27.289
be able to get $1,100 for.

00:04:27.289 --> 00:04:32.400
Because a bond fund is comprised of thousands
of individual bonds being constantly bought

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:36.189
and sold, bond funds go up and down, much
like a stock fund.

00:04:36.189 --> 00:04:39.319
But the waves are often half the height.

00:04:39.319 --> 00:04:43.819
This is why it’s common to find a retired
person using a substantial amount of bonds

00:04:43.819 --> 00:04:45.169
for their income needs.

00:04:45.169 --> 00:04:49.280
They’re less volatile, pay regular income,
and come with some guarantees.

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:54.720
Yeah! they can’t afford for the sky to fall
on them!

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:55.810
Get it?

00:04:55.810 --> 00:04:56.810
Skyfall?

00:04:56.810 --> 00:04:57.810
Too far?

00:04:57.810 --> 00:05:00.889
But what about younger people, should they
bother with bonds?

00:05:00.889 --> 00:05:01.889
Maybe!

00:05:01.889 --> 00:05:06.439
If you’re saving up for a short-term goal,
like a car or a down payment on a house, bond

00:05:06.439 --> 00:05:08.290
funds are a safer place to do it.

00:05:08.290 --> 00:05:11.689
You might not be Moonraking it in, but you won’t have the Spectre

00:05:11.689 --> 00:05:13.840
of losing it all hanging over your head.

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:19.520
As we’ve said before, there’s no Golden
Gun to investing, and bonds are no exception.

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:24.340
If you’re hoping to make more money than
a savings account offers but the volatility

00:05:24.340 --> 00:05:30.180
of the stock market scares the Living Daylights
out of you, bonds might be just what the

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:32.000
Dr. No ordered.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:34.740
And that's our cents...

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:36.740
...Two Cents.

00:05:36.740 --> 00:05:40.180
Hey! You should check out “Antarctic Extremes,”

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:43.260
a new mini-series from NOVA and PBS Digital Studios.

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:49.740
It’s a journey to Earth’s most remote laboratory—Antarctica— where science and survival meet.

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:55.240
Hosts Caitlin Saks and Arlo Pérez reveal a world that is sometimes harsh, sometimes hilarious,

00:05:55.240 --> 00:05:58.100
sometimes gross—but always thrilling.

00:05:58.100 --> 00:06:03.200
Find Antarctic Extremes on PBS Terra, PBS Digital Studios’ new science channel.

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:08.000
Check out the episode in the description below, and tell them Two Cents sent you!

00:06:08.380 --> 00:06:12.660
If you've ever invested in bonds, let us know how it worked out for you in the comments!

